The game now uses a standardized codename system for every character, attack, animation, and attack-created object. The goal is to make character files easier to understand, remove character-specific or inconsistent internal names, and allow the combat system to support different numbers of attacks without needing special-case code.

Each character is identified by a four-digit character ID. Character 0001, for example, uses filenames beginning with 0001_. The final number in an animation filename always represents the animation frame.

The standard animation format is:

characterID_animationName_frame

Examples:

0001_idle_1
0001_idle_2
0001_run_1
0001_jump_1
0001_fall_1
0001_land_1
0001_hurt_1
0001_midair_hurt_1

Shield animations use:

0001_prepshield_1
0001_shielding_1
0001_releaseshield_1

Movement or dash animations use the standardized mouvment codename:

0001_mouvment_1

The old attack names such as ba1, ba2, maba1, maba2, cba1, cba2, and uniqueba are removed. Attacks are now numbered directly.

The new attack names are:

attack1
attack2
attack3
attack4
attack5

Their animation files follow the same naming format:

0001_attack1_1
0001_attack1_2
0001_attack2_1
0001_attack3_1

Normal attacks that can be performed in the air use a separate midair_attack codename:

0001_midair_attack1_1
0001_midair_attack2_1
0001_midair_attack3_1

Every ordinary numbered attack should have an obvious midair version. The exception is a numbered attack that is a summon or a technique (see below): it is ground-only, so it has no midair version.

Characters may have a minimum of 2 and a maximum of 5 numbered attacks. Attack numbers must remain sequential. A character cannot have attack1, attack2, and attack4 while skipping attack3.

The extra_attack is separate from the numbered attack count. It replaces the old uniqueba system and is used for an additional character-specific action such as a projectile, throw, special utility move, or other unique attack.

Its animation is named:

0001_extra_attack_1

A character may therefore have five numbered attacks and still have an extra_attack.

Every numbered attack a character has is a numbered button the player presses directly. No held stance or modifier changes what a button does, and no numbered attack is reached through another button.

A numbered button is one of three kinds of move:

an ordinary attack: attackN on the ground, midair_attackN in the air
a summon: a detached entity the attack sends out, such as a clone
a technique: a multi-phase special move the fighter performs itself

attack1 and attack2 are always ordinary attacks. attack3, attack4 and attack5 may each be any of the three. A summon or a technique is ground-only, needs no midair version and has its own cooldown.

The exact button system depends only on the character’s attack count.

A character with 2 attacks has two attack buttons: attack1 and attack2.

A character with 3 attacks has three attack buttons: attack1, attack2 and attack3.

A character with 4 attacks has four attack buttons: attack1 to attack4.

A character with 5 attacks has five attack buttons: attack1 to attack5.

Whether attack3 or attack4 is an ordinary attack, a summon or a technique is determined by the character’s loadout, not by the attack name itself.

For example, one character may use attack3 as a completely normal third attack with its own midair version:

0001_attack3_1
0001_midair_attack3_1

Another character may instead make attack3 a summon on its own Attack 3 button. In that case attack3 does not automatically require a midair version, and the objects it creates use attack3_object.

Objects created by attacks also use the attack that created them as their codename. This applies to projectiles, clones, summons, deployables, effects, and similar attack-owned objects.

The standard format is:

characterID_attackName_object_frame

Examples:

0001_attack1_object_1
0001_attack3_object_1
0001_attack3_object_2
0001_extra_attack_object_1

This means an object should no longer be given an unrelated character-specific filename such as the name of a projectile, clone, technique, or fictional ability. The filename describes which gameplay attack owns the object.

For character 0001, the old attack system converts as follows:

ba1 → attack1
maba1 → midair_attack1
ba2 → attack2
maba2 → midair_attack2
cba1 → attack3
cba2 → attack4
uniqueba → extra_attack

Character 0001 therefore has four numbered attacks, each a button of its own. Attack1 and attack2 are ordinary attacks. Attack3 is a summon (the Clone Attack) and attack4 a technique (the Sphere Rush). The character’s previous unique attack becomes extra_attack.

Its routing is:

attack1 button → attack1 (midair_attack1 in the air)
attack2 button → attack2 (midair_attack2 in the air)
attack3 button → attack3, the clone summon
attack4 button → attack4, the Sphere Rush technique

Extra-attack button → extra_attack

Any projectile produced by the extra attack uses names such as:

0001_extra_attack_object_1

Any object created by attack3 uses:

0001_attack3_object_1

Any object created by attack4 uses:

0001_attack4_object_1

The important rule is that these names are now gameplay codenames, not move names. Display names such as “Punch,” “Kick,” “Clone Attack,” or other character-specific names may still be shown to players in menus and UI, but they must not replace the standardized internal identifiers.

This creates one consistent system across every character:

attack1 through attack5 describe numbered attacks.
midair_attack1 through midair_attack5 describe their normal airborne versions.
extra_attack describes the separate character-specific extra move.
each numbered attack is an ordinary attack, a summon or a technique, always on its own button.
attackX_object describes anything created by that attack.
The character ID identifies who owns the animation.
The final number identifies the frame.

As a result, character code, animation data, PNG filenames, controls, AI, mobile buttons, cooldowns, and attack-created objects can all refer to the same predictable naming system without relying on legacy terms such as BA, MABA, CBA, or Unique BA.


