## Attack count and button routing

Support 2–5 sequential numbered attack families. Ground and air versions are one family. Summons and techniques count toward this total. Preserve this document's existing decision that extra_attack is separate from the numbered limit; shield, movement, jump, down and transform are also not numbered attacks.

Every ordinary numbered attack must have an explicit, functional midair_attackN definition and animation. Only summons and techniques, which are ground-only, are exempt. An ordinary attack5 always needs midair_attack5. Do not satisfy validation with an idle pose, a missing clip or a silent ground-only fallback. Keep extra_attack's existing character-specific air/ground restrictions unless separately redesigned.

Every numbered attack is a button of its own on keyboard, gamepad and touch. There is no modifier, stance or replacement layer that reaches a numbered attack through another button.

| Numbered attacks | Numbered buttons |
| --- | --- |
| 2 | attack1, attack2 |
| 3 | attack1, attack2, attack3 |
| 4 | attack1, attack2, attack3, attack4 |
| 5 | attack1, attack2, attack3, attack4, attack5 |

A summon or technique button sends its own control codename (attack3 for 0001's Clone Attack, attack4 for its Sphere Rush), exactly like an ordinary attack button. Button visibility, slots and labels follow the same list.

Down is a directional input only: the fast fall and launch steering. It never enters a state, locks movement, blocks a Dash, speeds Energy or cooldowns, or changes what any button does. Menus keep their own Down navigation.

Use a shared, data-driven loadout resolver for combat, controllers, AI, button visibility and labels. Validate unknown IDs, gaps, more than five families, missing required air variants, missing objects, a summon or technique no button names, and unknown action types.

A summon or technique pressed while its own cooldown runs does nothing at all: no other attack in its place, and nothing kept for later. One that cannot happen (no opponent in play, missing art or data) fails cleanly the same way.

## Attack-created objects and existing mechanics

Every projectile, clone, summon, deployable or attack-owned effect uses its owning attack's object namespace. Air-specific objects use midair_attackN_object when owned by that air move. Runtime instance IDs may distinguish concurrent entities; they must not alter the asset frame suffix.

Preserve specialized projectile, summon and technique behavior classes. The naming requirement does not require deleting those abstractions or turning every entity into a generic projectile. If an attack creates several visually distinct objects, define collision-free child keys beneath its object namespace and document that extension; never reuse the final frame number as an object-type discriminator.

For character 0001, preserve:

- attack1 and midair_attack1: the existing punch and airborne slash.
- attack2 and midair_attack2: the existing kick/launcher and airborne downward attack.
- attack3: the clone summon on its own Attack 3 button. Its appearance/disappearance cloud uses attack3_object. Preserve reversed disappearance playback and the clone's explicit reuse of the owner's attack1 or midair_attack2 clips and hit data.
- attack4: the Sphere Rush technique on its own Attack 4 button. Preserve all form, rush, confirm, explosion, recovery and whiff phases; the 12 pose frames and 11 object frames; hand offsets; target binding; tick damage; explosion timing; interrupts and cooldown.
- extra_attack: the shuriken throw, with its object release timing, flight, hitbox, lifetime and animation.

A summon that reuses owner poses need not duplicate the PNGs to rename their pixels. Keep that reuse explicit in its definition while naming summon-owned objects and effects after attack3.

